Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Abstract", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"9a224e66-1d41-4f1e-823d-4d64a2d53e0d"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "A feedforward neural network is a chain of simple steps: multiply by a \
matrix, add a vector, apply an S-shaped function, and repeat. This notebook \
builds such a network from ordinary Wolfram Language functions, one step at a \
time, and shows both the algebra and the computing behind each step. It opens \
with the smallest network that has every part, one input, one hidden neuron \
and one output, and carries out one learning step by hand: the forward pass, \
the loss, the backward pass by the chain rule, and the update, with every \
number written out and a Manipulate that steps through the computation on a \
diagram. Then a single neuron computes the logical AND and OR; a layer of \
neurons is one matrix product, and processing many inputs at once turns it \
into a matrix-matrix product that runs about 35 times faster here than a \
loop. XOR cannot be computed by one neuron, which is proved, but a 2-2-1 \
network computes it, and a picture of its hidden layer shows why. The loss \
functions, the backpropagation equations and gradient descent are derived and \
programmed, and the gradients are checked against symbolic differentiation \
and finite differences. Four trainings follow, each shown with a Manipulate \
or an animation. A network learns XOR from random weights, and a single \
neuron learns the rule F = 1.8 C + 32 from eight familiar temperatures, with \
its path over the loss landscape. Then two networks learn to keep a physical \
system moving. A 4-8-1 network learns to balance a stick on a cart by \
imitating a force-limited LQR controller: in the animation the untrained \
network drops the stick within a second, and after 100 epochs it holds it \
upright, even when the stick is hit. A 1-6-1 network learns the force law of \
a pendulum from 200 noisy measurements; run as a simulator, it keeps the \
pendulum swinging with the right period, 8.34 s against the true 8.35 s, \
where the textbook small-angle law gives 6.28 s, and it is proved that such a \
learned model conserves energy. Every network is also drawn as a diagram with \
its trained weights. Finally the same networks are built with NetChain and \
NetTrain, and the weights of a trained NetChain, run through our own code, \
reproduce its outputs to ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SuperscriptBox["10", 
    RowBox[{"-", "7"}]], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "3d4f0e8f-5f1c-4200-8775-8f1b4c90286b"],
 ": the framework does exactly the algebra of this notebook."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"9b9c3a9a-26cf-4aa4-a85d-0a3c26ae681f"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Keywords: ",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 "neuron; activation function; logistic sigmoid; matrix form; batch \
computation; XOR; hidden layer; linear separability; mean squared error; \
softmax; cross-entropy; backpropagation; chain rule; gradient descent; \
learning rate; Adam; cart-pole; inverted pendulum; LQR; imitation learning; \
learned dynamics; energy conservation; Manipulate; NetChain; Wolfram \
Language"
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"e1b2993f-e9de-4ead-a462-26e1b3c86af3"],

Cell["\<\
Every code cell starts with a comment such as (*c.9*) giving its index, and \
the cells are meant to run in order from c.0. The notebook keeps its \
variables in a private context (CellContext -> Notebook). The interactive \
cells, c.0.4, c.13, c.14, c.17, c.20, c.21 and c.23, store their data with \
SaveDefinitions -> True or as animation frames, so they still work after the \
notebook is closed and reopened. References of the form Figure M3 or Theorem \
M2 point to the notebook S Shape Functions Theory [1]. Numbers in square \
brackets refer to the list of references at the end.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"c7abf3d6-d8df-4d5f-82d5-70b0005da6d0"]
}, Open  ]],

